  {:title
   "Undo snapshot recorded for every keypress, including pure navigation; recursive control dispatch double-records",
   :file
   "packages/terminal-ui/src/cljs/eta_mu/terminal_ui/infra/input_editor.cljs",
   :detail
   "record-undo! runs unconditionally at apply-key entry (line 95), so cursor moves, history recall, and yank all push buffer snapshots; ctrl-_ after N navigation keys pops N identical buffers before reaching the actual text change. Control keys dispatched recursively (lines 141-148, e.g. ctrl-p -> [:history-prev]) push two snapshots for one keypress. Undo-stack itself is correct (clone-on-push, domain/undo_stack.cljs); this is granularity polish, not a DoD item — candidate follow-up, not a promotion blocker.",
   :severity "nit",
   :card "input-editor",
   :lens "dod-coverage",
   :survives false,
   :reasons
   ["The factual core checks out — record-undo! does run unconditionally at apply-key entry (line 95), and the recursive :control dispatch at lines 141-148 (ctrl-p → [:history-prev] etc.) does push a second identical snapshot per keypress, so navigation keys do clutter the undo stack with no-op entries. But the finding defeats itself as a review gate: the reviewer explicitly classifies it as a 'nit', states 'this is granularity polish, not a DoD item — candidate follow-up, not a promotion blocker', and confirms the undo-stack mechanism itself is correct (clone-on-push in domain/undo_stack.cljs). Checking the card's Definition of Done (cursor movement tests, history recall test, multi-line test, pty verification, no raw interop outside extern.*, green test/kondo gates), undo granularity appears nowhere; the scope item was to 'wire' the already-existing domain.undo-stack, which the implementation comment records as done with 6 passing fake-terminal tests and all gates green. The practical impact is benign: undoing past a navigation key pops an identical buffer (no visible effect, one extra ctrl-_), and actual text changes remain reachable — no data loss, no corruption, no violated contract. Per skeptic criteria 3 and 4, a self-declared non-blocking nit outside the card's delivered scope cannot gate a review→done promotion; it belongs in a follow-up card if anyone cares to file one."
    "The factual mechanics are accurate — record-undo! at input_editor.cljs:95 runs before the key dispatch case, so navigation/history/yank keys do push buffer snapshots, and the recursive control dispatch (lines 141-148, e.g. ctrl-p → (apply-key state [:history-prev])) records twice per keypress (both snapshots identical since both precede mutation; undo_stack.cljs has no dedup, just conj-on-push). However, the finding fails the promotion-gating test on its own terms: the reviewer explicitly classifies it as a 'nit', states 'this is granularity polish, not a DoD item — candidate follow-up, not a promotion blocker', and concedes the undo-stack itself is correct. Nothing in the card's Definition of Done (cursor-movement tests, history test, multi-line test, TTY wiring, layering, green suites) requires undo coalescing, and the card's comment history documents full DoD delivery with all gates green. The behavior is functionally correct — undo works, just at finer granularity than ideal — so this is at most a follow-up polish ticket, not a finding that can lawfully gate a review→done promotion."]}]}
